{
  "id": "exponents-and-surds",
  "title": "Exponents and Surds",
  "term": 1,
  "summary": "Rational exponents and surds: simplifying, rationalising denominators, exponential equations and equations with surds.",
  "notes": [
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Rational exponents" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "text": "The exponent laws from Grade 10 also hold for **rational** (fractional) exponents. A rational exponent is linked to roots:" },
    { "type": "formula", "latex": "a^{\\frac{1}{n}} = \\sqrt[n]{a} \\qquad a^{\\frac{m}{n}} = \\left(\\sqrt[n]{a}\\right)^m = \\sqrt[n]{a^m}", "caption": "$a > 0$, $m, n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $n > 0$" },
    { "type": "table", "caption": "Exponent laws", "headers": ["Law", "Example"], "rows": [
      ["$a^m \\times a^n = a^{m+n}$", "$x^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\cdot x^{\\frac{3}{2}} = x^2$"],
      ["$a^m \\div a^n = a^{m-n}$", "$\\dfrac{8^{\\frac{5}{3}}}{8^{\\frac{2}{3}}} = 8$"],
      ["$(a^m)^n = a^{mn}$", "$\\left(27^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\right)^2 = 9$"],
      ["$a^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{a^n}$", "$16^{-\\frac{1}{2}} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$"],
      ["$a^0 = 1$", "$(3x)^0 = 1$"]
    ] },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 1", "problem": "Simplify without a calculator: $\\left(\\dfrac{27}{8}\\right)^{-\\frac{2}{3}}$", "steps": [
      "$= \\left(\\dfrac{8}{27}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}$ (negative exponent: flip the fraction)",
      "$= \\left(\\dfrac{2^3}{3^3}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}} = \\dfrac{2^2}{3^2}$",
      "$= \\dfrac{4}{9}$"
    ], "answer": "$\\frac{4}{9}$" },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Surds" },
    { "type": "list", "items": [
      "$\\sqrt{ab} = \\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}$ and $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{a}{b}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{a}}{\\sqrt{b}}$ for $a, b \\geq 0$.",
      "Simplify by taking out perfect-square factors: $\\sqrt{72} = \\sqrt{36 \\times 2} = 6\\sqrt{2}$.",
      "Add and subtract like surds only: $3\\sqrt{5} + 2\\sqrt{5} = 5\\sqrt{5}$."
    ] },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 2: simplifying", "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{50} + \\sqrt{18} - \\sqrt{8}$", "steps": ["$= 5\\sqrt{2} + 3\\sqrt{2} - 2\\sqrt{2}$", "$= 6\\sqrt{2}$"], "answer": "$6\\sqrt{2}$" },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Rationalising the denominator" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "text": "To remove a surd from a denominator, multiply the numerator and denominator by the surd, or by the **conjugate** for a two-term denominator: the conjugate of $a + \\sqrt{b}$ is $a - \\sqrt{b}$." },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 3", "problem": "Rationalise: (a) $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{3}}$ (b) $\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{5} - 1}$", "steps": [
      "(a) $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\dfrac{6\\sqrt{3}}{3} = 2\\sqrt{3}$",
      "(b) $\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{5} - 1} \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5} + 1}{\\sqrt{5} + 1} = \\dfrac{4(\\sqrt{5} + 1)}{5 - 1} = \\sqrt{5} + 1$"
    ], "answer": "(a) $2\\sqrt{3}$ (b) $\\sqrt{5} + 1$" },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Exponential equations" },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 4", "problem": "Solve for $x$: (a) $x^{\\frac{3}{2}} = 27$ (b) $2^{x+1} + 2^x = 24$", "steps": [
      "(a) Raise both sides to the power $\\frac{2}{3}$: $x = 27^{\\frac{2}{3}} = 9$.",
      "(b) Factorise: $2^x(2 + 1) = 24$, so $2^x = 8 = 2^3$ and $x = 3$."
    ], "answer": "(a) $x = 9$ (b) $x = 3$" },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Surd equations" },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 5", "problem": "Solve: $\\sqrt{x + 7} = x + 1$", "steps": [
      "Square both sides: $x + 7 = x^2 + 2x + 1$.",
      "$x^2 + x - 6 = 0$, so $(x + 3)(x - 2) = 0$: $x = -3$ or $x = 2$.",
      "Check: $x = 2$: $\\sqrt{9} = 3 = 2 + 1$ ✓. $x = -3$: $\\sqrt{4} = 2 \\neq -2$ ✗.",
      "So $x = 2$ only."
    ], "answer": "$x = 2$" },
    { "type": "callout", "label": "Exam tip", "text": "Squaring both sides can introduce **extraneous** (false) solutions. Always check every answer in the original surd equation." }
  ],
